<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shiban</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shiban was a Mongol prince, a son of Jochi and grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, who fought in Batu's western campaign against the Rus', <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in the late 1230s and early 1240s. He was rewarded with lands east of the Ural mountains as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. His descendants, the Shibanids, later founded the Uzbek Khanate and the Khanate of Sibir and, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaybanids">Shaybanids</a>, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shiban.jpg" alt="Shiban" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous illumination · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Mohi_1241.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
